 PCK:,NAME,
C===========================================================
C pck(p) - pack six characters into one word.
C
C Calling convention (single argument, in ACC):
C   ACC = p, the word address of the first of six characters
C   M13 = return link
C
C   int pck(int *) assembler;
C
C Result:
C   ACC = the six characters at p packed into one word, the
C         first of them in the most significant byte.
C
C This is the PCK standard procedure as a function: PCK sets
C M12 to the address of a CHAR lvalue, calls P/PA and stores
C the word P/PA leaves in the accumulator, so the whole of the
C work is P/PA's and the address is all it wants.  An unpacked
C character array gives every element a word of its own, which
C is why the argument is an INT * rather than a CHAR *, a
C CHAR * being a byte index into flat memory.
C
C P/PA returns the packed word through M13, which still holds
C the caller's link, so the jump into it is the return as well.
C===========================================================
 P/PA:          ,SUBP,
                ,NTR ,7      . ABI requirement
                ,ATI ,12     . M12 := p, the address P/PA packs from
                ,UJ  ,P/PA   . tail call: P/PA returns the word to our caller
                ,END ,
